{"PubTator3":[{"_id":"36475449|PMC9867944","id":"9867944","infons":{},"passages":[{"infons":{"name_3":"surname:Li;given-names:Yanning","name_2":"surname:Jiang;given-names:Dan","name_1":"surname:Yang;given-names:Shaoqi","name_0":"surname:Zhang;given-names:Yanting","issue":"1","year":"2023","article-id_publisher-id":"BSR20221546","article-id_doi":"10.1042/BSR20221546","type":"front","elocation-id":"BSR20221546","volume":"43","license":"This is an open access article published by Portland Press Limited on behalf of the Biochemical Society and distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License 4.0 (CC BY).","section_type":"TITLE","kwd":"check point immunotherapy Nanobody PD-1 phage display library","name_9":"surname:Xu;given-names:Guangxian","name_8":"surname:Zhang;given-names:Aijun","name_7":"surname:Wang;given-names:Hongxia","name_6":"surname:Li;given-names:Guangqi","name_5":"surname:Wang;given-names:Liyan","name_4":"surname:Ma;given-names:Shuo","article-id_pmc":"9867944","article-id_pmid":"36475449"},"offset":0,"text":"Screening and identification of an anti-PD-1 nanobody with antitumor activity","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"ABSTRACT","type":"abstract_title_1"},"offset":78,"text":"Abstract","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"ABSTRACT","type":"abstract"},"offset":87,"text":"Blocking of programmed death protein 1 (PD-1) or its ligand PD-L1 with corresponding antibody to enhance T-cell response and mediate antitumor activity has been successfully applied in clinical practice. Several immune checkpoint inhibitors including monoclonal antibodies (mAbs) targeting PD-1 have been approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in cancer immunotherapy. However, the application of traditional antibodies has limited due to their drawbacks of large molecular weight (MW) and low tissue penetration. As the high specificity and strong tissue penetration of nanobodies (Nbs), efforts have been taken to develop Nbs for cancer therapy. Herein, we aim to screen a specific Nb against human PD-1 derived from a naive camel Nb phage display library and further study its biological characteristic and antitumor activity. Finally, an anti-PD-1 Nb with high specificity and affinity was screened and generated, its cytotoxicity and antitumor effect was also confirmed in vitro and in vivo. All of these indicate that the anti-PD-1 Nb may provide an alternative and appealing therapeutic agent for cancer immunotherapy.","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"19583","infons":{"identifier":"5133","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"3681","valid":true,"normalized":[5133],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":5133,"biotype":"gene","name":"PDCD1","accession":"@GENE_PDCD1"},"text":"programmed death protein 1","locations":[{"offset":99,"length":26}]},{"id":"19584","infons":{"identifier":"29126","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"8560","valid":true,"normalized":[29126],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":29126,"biotype":"gene","name":"CD274","accession":"@GENE_CD274"},"text":"PD-L1","locations":[{"offset":147,"length":5}]},{"id":"19585","infons":{"identifier":"9606","type":"Species","valid":true,"normalized":[9606],"database":"ncbi_taxonomy","normalized_id":9606,"biotype":"species","name":"9606","accession":null},"text":"human","locations":[{"offset":794,"length":5}]},{"id":"19586","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D009369","type":"Disease","valid":true,"normalized":["D009369"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D009369","biotype":"disease","name":"Neoplasms","accession":"@DISEASE_Neoplasms"},"text":"cancer","locations":[{"offset":731,"length":6}]},{"id":"19587","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D064420","type":"Disease","valid":true,"normalized":["D064420"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D064420","biotype":"disease","name":"Drug-Related Side Effects and Adverse Reactions","accession":"@DISEASE_Drug_Related_Side_Effects_and_Adverse_Reactions"},"text":"cytotoxicity","locations":[{"offset":1021,"length":12}]},{"id":"19588","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D009369","type":"Disease","valid":true,"normalized":["D009369"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D009369","biotype":"disease","name":"Neoplasms","accession":"@DISEASE_Neoplasms"},"text":"cancer","locations":[{"offset":1203,"length":6}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"INTRO","type":"title_1"},"offset":1226,"text":"Introduction","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"INTRO","type":"paragraph"},"offset":1239,"text":"Immunotherapy has revolutionized the field of oncology. Immune checkpoint inhibition has become one of the most important strategies in cancer treatment. Programmed death protein 1 (PD-1) interacts with its ligand PD-L1 overexpressed on malignant tumor cells, leading to tumor immune evasion. Blocking PD-1 or PD-L1 to enhance T-cell response and mediate antitumor activity has been successfully applied in clinical practice. So far, several immune checkpoint inhibitors, including monoclonal antibodies (mAbs) targeting PD-1 or PD-L1, have been approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for cancer therapy. While most patients benefit from the therapy, some do not respond to treatment and may develop severe side effects. In addition, the application of traditional antibodies is affected by poor stability, large molecular weight (MW), low tissue penetration, and immune-related adverse events.","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"19590","infons":{"identifier":"5133","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"3681","valid":true,"normalized":[5133],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":5133,"biotype":"gene","name":"PDCD1","accession":"@GENE_PDCD1"},"text":"Programmed death protein 1","locations":[{"offset":1393,"length":26}]},{"id":"19591","infons":{"identifier":"29126","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"8560","valid":true,"normalized":[29126],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":29126,"biotype":"gene","name":"CD274","accession":"@GENE_CD274"},"text":"PD-L1","locations":[{"offset":1453,"length":5}]},{"id":"19592","infons":{"identifier":"29126","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"8560","valid":true,"normalized":[29126],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":29126,"biotype":"gene","name":"CD274","accession":"@GENE_CD274"},"text":"PD-L1","locations":[{"offset":1549,"length":5}]},{"id":"19593","infons":{"identifier":"29126","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"8560","valid":true,"normalized":[29126],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":29126,"biotype":"gene","name":"CD274","accession":"@GENE_CD274"},"text":"PD-L1","locations":[{"offset":1768,"length":5}]},{"id":"19594","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D009369","type":"Disease","valid":true,"normalized":["D009369"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D009369","biotype":"disease","name":"Neoplasms","accession":"@DISEASE_Neoplasms"},"text":"tumor","locations":[{"offset":1510,"length":5}]},{"id":"19595","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D009369","type":"Disease","valid":true,"normalized":["D009369"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D009369","biotype":"disease","name":"Neoplasms","accession":"@DISEASE_Neoplasms"},"text":"cancer","locations":[{"offset":1375,"length":6}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"INTRO","type":"paragraph"},"offset":2146,"text":"Nanobodies (Nbs), also known as the variable domain of the heavy chain of the heavy-chain-only antibody (VHH), with small size (~15 kDa, 4 nm long, and 2.5 nm wide), high specificity, and strong tissue penetration, may provide an answer to these concerns. Nbs are composed of four framework regions (FR1-4) alternated with three complementarity determining regions (CDR1-3). The amino acid mutations in FR2 render the overall structure more hydrophilic, leading to higher solubility and possibly stability than conventional antibodies. In addition, the extended CDR3 loop can recognize the hidden or buried epitopes, which cannot be recognized by traditional antibodies. Due to the high similarity with the human VH sequence, Nbs have been associated with limited immunogenicity risks. Studies have established the other hallmarks of Nbs, including high stability and chemical resistance. Therefore, Nbs are suitable for fast selection from immune or naive libraries and easy cloning based on the established display platform technology. Additionally, Nbs could be easily produced in an appropriate expression system like E. coli, S. cerevisiae, or P. pastoris, with a favorable, cost-effective creation. A recent study showed that an anti-PD-L1-specific Nb fused to human IgG1 Fc has remarkable antitumor activity in vivo and has been currently tested in clinical trials. Another study demonstrated that a novel anti-PD-1 Nb expressed in yeast could block the PD-1/PD-L1 axis with potential immunotherapeutic effects. These properties make Nb conducive to be a potential reagent in cancer immunotherapy.","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"19599","infons":{"identifier":"29126","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"8560","valid":true,"normalized":[29126],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":29126,"biotype":"gene","name":"CD274","accession":"@GENE_CD274"},"text":"PD-L1","locations":[{"offset":3386,"length":5}]},{"id":"19600","infons":{"identifier":"16017","type":"Gene","valid":true,"normalized":[16017],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":16017,"biotype":"gene","name":"Ighg1","accession":"@GENE_IGHG1"},"text":"IgG1","locations":[{"offset":3419,"length":4}]},{"id":"19601","infons":{"identifier":"29126","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"8560","valid":true,"normalized":[29126],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":29126,"biotype":"gene","name":"CD274","accession":"@GENE_CD274"},"text":"PD-L1","locations":[{"offset":3612,"length":5}]},{"id":"19602","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"Nb","locations":[{"offset":3687,"length":2}]},{"id":"19603","infons":{"identifier":"9606","type":"Species","valid":true,"normalized":[9606],"database":"ncbi_taxonomy","normalized_id":9606,"biotype":"species","name":"9606","accession":null},"text":"human","locations":[{"offset":3413,"length":5}]},{"id":"19604","infons":{"identifier":"562","type":"Species","valid":true,"normalized":[562],"database":"ncbi_taxonomy","normalized_id":562,"biotype":"species","name":"562","accession":null},"text":"E. coli","locations":[{"offset":3268,"length":7}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"INTRO","type":"paragraph"},"offset":3758,"text":"In the present study, a specific Nb-targeting PD-1 was screened from a naive camel Nb phage display library. The anti-PD-1 Nb was fused to human IgG1 Fc and expressed in a prokaryotic expression system. Its antitumor activity was then tested in vitro and in vivo.","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"19612","infons":{"identifier":"16017","type":"Gene","valid":true,"normalized":[16017],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":16017,"biotype":"gene","name":"Ighg1","accession":"@GENE_IGHG1"},"text":"IgG1","locations":[{"offset":3903,"length":4}]},{"id":"19613","infons":{"identifier":"9606","type":"Species","valid":true,"normalized":[9606],"database":"ncbi_taxonomy","normalized_id":9606,"biotype":"species","name":"9606","accession":null},"text":"human","locations":[{"offset":3897,"length":5}]},{"id":"19614","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"Nb","locations":[{"offset":3791,"length":2}]},{"id":"19615","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"Nb","locations":[{"offset":3841,"length":2}]},{"id":"19616","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"Nb","locations":[{"offset":3881,"length":2}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"METHODS","type":"title_1"},"offset":4023,"text":"Materials and methods","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"METHODS","type":"title_2"},"offset":4045,"text":"Generation of the VHH phage display library","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"METHODS","type":"paragraph"},"offset":4089,"text":"The total RNA was extracted from the spleen of a healthy Bactrian camel and was well preserved in our lab using Trizol (Invitrogen, U.S.A.). The study has been approved by Medical Research Ethics Review Committee of General Hospital of Ningxia Medical University (KYLL-2021-195). The mRNAs were then transcribed to cDNA using reverse transcription PCR (ThermoScript RT-PCR kit, Thermo, U.S.A.). Then, the VHH fragments were amplified by two-step nested PCR using specific primers. The primers of the first PCR were CALL001 and CALL002 (Supplementary Table 1). Amplicons spanning the VHH-CH2 exons with a size of 700 bp were generated during the first PCR with the common primers CALL001 and CALL002. Then, products (700 bp) were extracted from the 1.5% agarose gel as a template. The second PCR amplification was performed with VHH-Forward and VHH-Reverse primers (Supplementary Table 1). The final products (400 bp) were extracted from 2% agarose gel purification. As the second primer only coped with the VHH fragment, the fragments containing CH1 were removed. Next, a phage vector pCANTAB-5E and the amplified PCR products were digested with NotI and SfiI restriction enzymes (Takara, Japan) and then ligated with T4 DNA ligase enzyme (NEB, England) at a molar ratio of 1:3 at 16 C for 1 h at 4 C. The recombinant vector was electrotransformed into E. coli TG1 cells.","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"19617","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D012685","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D012685"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D012685","biotype":"chemical","name":"Sepharose","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Sepharose"},"text":"agarose","locations":[{"offset":4842,"length":7}]},{"id":"19618","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:C411644","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["C411644"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"C411644","biotype":"chemical","name":"trizol","accession":"@CHEMICAL_trizol"},"text":"Trizol","locations":[{"offset":4201,"length":6}]},{"id":"19619","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D012685","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D012685"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D012685","biotype":"chemical","name":"Sepharose","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Sepharose"},"text":"agarose","locations":[{"offset":5029,"length":7}]},{"id":"19620","infons":{"identifier":"562","type":"Species","valid":true,"normalized":[562],"database":"ncbi_taxonomy","normalized_id":562,"biotype":"species","name":"562","accession":null},"text":"E. coli","locations":[{"offset":5442,"length":7}]},{"id":"19621","infons":{"type":"Species","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"species"},"text":"camel","locations":[{"offset":4155,"length":5}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"METHODS","type":"paragraph"},"offset":5463,"text":"To produce phage, the library was cultured in 50-ml 2xYT medium (containing 0.1% glucose and 100 mug/ml ampicillin) at 37 C (OD = 0.5) and then incubated with 1012 M13O7 helper phage at 37 C for 30 min. To make VHH repertoire displayed on phage, library cells were collected and cultivated in 200 ml 2xYT medium supplemented with 100 mug/ml ampicillin and 50 mug/ml kanamycin at 30 C overnight. Samples were then collected and centrifuged at 9000 rpm for 10 min. The supernatant was mixed with 1/5 (v/v) PEG8000/NaCl for 8 h. Finally, the phage pellet was resuspended in PBS for later use. The size of the library was calculated by counting the number of colonies after gradient dilution. Finally, 20 colonies were randomly picked to estimate the VHH insertion rate of VHH genes by PCR amplification.","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"19622","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D007854","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D007854"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D007854","biotype":"chemical","name":"Lead","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Lead"},"text":"PBS","locations":[{"offset":6034,"length":3}]},{"id":"19623","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:C000595216","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["C000595216"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"C000595216","biotype":"chemical","name":"polyethylene glycol 8000","accession":"@CHEMICAL_polyethylene_glycol_8000"},"text":"PEG8000","locations":[{"offset":5967,"length":7}]},{"id":"19624","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D005947","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D005947"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D005947","biotype":"chemical","name":"Glucose","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Glucose"},"text":"glucose","locations":[{"offset":5544,"length":7}]},{"id":"19625","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"2xYT medium","locations":[{"offset":5515,"length":11}]},{"id":"19626","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D000667","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D000667"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D000667","biotype":"chemical","name":"Ampicillin","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Ampicillin"},"text":"ampicillin","locations":[{"offset":5804,"length":10}]},{"id":"19627","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D000667","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D000667"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D000667","biotype":"chemical","name":"Ampicillin","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Ampicillin"},"text":"ampicillin","locations":[{"offset":5567,"length":10}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"METHODS","type":"title_2"},"offset":6269,"text":"Screening of PD-1 Nb by phage display","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"METHODS","type":"paragraph"},"offset":6307,"text":"Based on the successfully constructed VHH library, biotinylated human PD-1 antigen (Acro biosystems, Newark) was used to select the specific coding anti-PD-1 VHH by phage display and soluble biopanning. First, 500 mul phage and 5-mug-biotinylated hPD-1 antigen were mixed in a 1.5-ml centrifuge tube and rotated for 1 h at room temperature. Then, 50-mul streptavidin magnetic beads (Invitrogen, U.S.A.) were added and incubated for 30 min. The phage was eluted with 800 mul Tris-Hcl (pH 2.7) for 8 min and neutralized with 100 mul 0.2 M glycine (pH 9.1) after repeated washing. After that, the neutralized eluent was added to TG1 (OD600 = 0.5), incubated at 37 C for 30 min, and cultured on 2YT plates (supplemented with 2% glucose, 100 mug/ml ampicillin, and 50 mug/ml kanamycin) at 37 C overnight. The resulting phage was scraped for the next round of panning, in which the antigen protein was reduced to 3 mug. Ninety-six colonies were picked from the third- and fourth-round panning and cultured separately in 96 plates, then identified by Phage-ELISA. The supernatant of the colonies was collected and added to the plate wells, which were coated with human PD-1 protein (Acro biosystems, Newark) in advance for 1 h, followed by incubation with anti-M13/HRP (1:5000) antibody for another 1 h. The hydrogen peroxide and TMB were added to each plate well after repeated washing. The absorbance was read using Microplate Reader (Thermo, U.S.A.) at 450 nm. Finally, the positive colonies were sequenced and transformed into an amino acid sequence. CDR and FR were numbered according to IMGT. The three-dimensional structure of the Nbs was predicted using a Swiss model. The MW and hydropathicity of the Nbs were evaluated using an online software of the ExPAsy-Protparam Tool.","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"19631","infons":{"identifier":"5133","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"3681","valid":true,"normalized":[5133],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":5133,"biotype":"gene","name":"PDCD1","accession":"@GENE_PDCD1"},"text":"hPD-1","locations":[{"offset":6554,"length":5}]},{"id":"19632","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D006851","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D006851"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D006851","biotype":"chemical","name":"Hydrochloric Acid","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Hydrochloric_Acid"},"text":"Hcl","locations":[{"offset":6786,"length":3}]},{"id":"19633","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D005947","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D005947"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D005947","biotype":"chemical","name":"Glucose","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Glucose"},"text":"glucose","locations":[{"offset":7031,"length":7}]},{"id":"19634","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"Tris","locations":[{"offset":6781,"length":4}]},{"id":"19635","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D000667","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D000667"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D000667","biotype":"chemical","name":"Ampicillin","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Ampicillin"},"text":"ampicillin","locations":[{"offset":7051,"length":10}]},{"id":"19636","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D005998","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D005998"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D005998","biotype":"chemical","name":"Glycine","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Glycine"},"text":"glycine","locations":[{"offset":6844,"length":7}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"METHODS","type":"title_2"},"offset":8086,"text":"Construction and expression of recombinant PD-1 Nb","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"METHODS","type":"paragraph"},"offset":8137,"text":"Two PD-1 Nb sequences (C11 and C12) were obtained after comparing the positive clones' sequences using NCBI. Human IgG1 Fc gene fragment was linked to the PD-1 Nb sequence by PCR-based accurate synthesis. The anti-PD-1 Nb C12-Fc sequence was cloned into plasmid pCZN1 and then transformed into TOP 10 E. coli strain, while C11 Nb failed to ligate to pCZN1. The recombinant pCZN1-PD-1 Nb C12-Fc was then transformed into E. coli Arctic-Express and cultured at 37 C in LB medium containing ampicillin (50 mug/ml), after which it was induced with 0.5-mM isopropyl-beta-d-thiogalactoside (IPTG). As the target protein (anti-PD-1 Nb-Fc) was mainly present in the precipitate (inclusion body), denaturing and refolding assay was performed. The protein was purified by Ni-NTA spin columns affinity chromatography (Novagen, Germany) and ultrafiltrated to remove the imidazole molecules and dialyzed into PBS solution. Ultimately, the purified anti-PD-1 Nb-Fc was checked by sodium dodecyl sulfate-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE).","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"19641","infons":{"identifier":"16017","type":"Gene","valid":true,"normalized":[16017],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":16017,"biotype":"gene","name":"Ighg1","accession":"@GENE_IGHG1"},"text":"IgG1","locations":[{"offset":8252,"length":4}]},{"id":"19642","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D012967","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D012967"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D012967","biotype":"chemical","name":"Sodium Dodecyl Sulfate","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Sodium_Dodecyl_Sulfate"},"text":"SDS","locations":[{"offset":9162,"length":3}]},{"id":"19643","infons":{"identifier":"9606","type":"Species","valid":true,"normalized":[9606],"database":"ncbi_taxonomy","normalized_id":9606,"biotype":"species","name":"9606","accession":null},"text":"Human","locations":[{"offset":8246,"length":5}]},{"id":"19644","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"Nb","locations":[{"offset":8464,"length":2}]},{"id":"19645","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"IPTG","locations":[{"offset":8722,"length":4}]},{"id":"19646","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D007854","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D007854"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D007854","biotype":"chemical","name":"Lead","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Lead"},"text":"PBS","locations":[{"offset":9033,"length":3}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"METHODS","type":"title_2"},"offset":9174,"text":"Affinity measurement","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"METHODS","type":"paragraph"},"offset":9195,"text":"The affinity of obtained anti-PD-1 Nb-Fc was measured by surface plasmon resonance (SPR) using Biacore 8K (Healthcare Life Science, GE). First, the anti-PD-1 Nb-Fc (10 mug/ml) was immobilized on sensor chip CM5 (GE) to 300 RU with a flow speed of 10 mul/min. Then, the human PD-1 antigen (Acro biosystems) was injected into the experimental channel at a flow rate of 30 L/min with a concentration from 100 to 0.391 nM. The association and dissociation time was 120 and 300 s, respectively. The dissociation constant was calculated by Biacore Insight Evaluation Software (GE).","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"19661","infons":{"identifier":"9606","type":"Species","valid":true,"normalized":[9606],"database":"ncbi_taxonomy","normalized_id":9606,"biotype":"species","name":"9606","accession":null},"text":"human","locations":[{"offset":9464,"length":5}]},{"id":"19662","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"CM5","locations":[{"offset":9402,"length":3}]},{"id":"19663","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"Nb-Fc","locations":[{"offset":9353,"length":5}]},{"id":"19664","infons":{"identifier":"-","type":"Chemical","valid":false,"normalized_id":null,"biotype":"chemical"},"text":"Nb-Fc","locations":[{"offset":9230,"length":5}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"FIG","file":"bsr-43-bsr20221546-g1.jpg","id":"F1","type":"fig_title_caption"},"offset":18744,"text":"Generation of VHH phage display library, screening, and development of anti-PD-1 Nb","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"FIG","file":"bsr-43-bsr20221546-g2.jpg","id":"F2","type":"fig_title_caption"},"offset":19770,"text":"Screening of anti-PD-1 Nb","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"FIG","file":"bsr-43-bsr20221546-g2.jpg","id":"F2","type":"fig_caption"},"offset":19796,"text":"(A) The positive colonies from the third and fourth panning round were further selected by Phage-ELISA. The supernatant of the colonies was added to the plates coated with the PD-1 antigen, and the anti-M13/HRP was added as the secondary antibody. The positive candidates with absorbance greater than two times that of the negative control (PBS) were selected. (B) The amino acid sequences of anti-PD-1 Nbs C11 and C12. Amino acids position of CDR and FR are numbered according to IMGT. CDR1, CDR2, and CDR3 are shown in blue, red, and green, respectively. (C) The 3D structure of the Nbs was predicted using a Swiss model. (D) The MW and the grand average of hydropathicity (GRAVY) of the Nbs were also evaluated using an online software of ExPAsy-Protparam Tool.","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"19778","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D007854","type":"Chemical","valid":true,"normalized":["D007854"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D007854","biotype":"chemical","name":"Lead","accession":"@CHEMICAL_Lead"},"text":"PBS","locations":[{"offset":20137,"length":3}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"COMP_INT","type":"title_1"},"offset":34128,"text":"Competing Interests","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"ABBR","type":"title"},"offset":34801,"text":"Abbreviations","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"section_type":"REF","type":"title"},"offset":35495,"text":"References","sentences":[],"annotations":[],"relations":[]}],"relations":[{"id":"R1","infons":{"score":"0.9991","role1":{"identifier":"MESH:D009369","type":"Disease","valid":true,"normalized":["D009369"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D009369","biotype":"disease","name":"Neoplasms","accession":"@DISEASE_Neoplasms"},"role2":{"identifier":"5133","type":"Gene","valid":true,"normalized":[5133],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":5133,"biotype":"gene","name":"PDCD1","accession":"@GENE_PDCD1"},"type":"Association"},"nodes":[]}],"pmid":36475449,"pmcid":"PMC9867944","meta":{},"date":"2022-12-07T00:00:00Z","journal":"Biosci Rep","authors":["Zhang Y","Yang S","Jiang D","Li Y","Ma S","Wang L","Li G","Wang H","Zhang A","Xu G"],"relations_display":[{"name":"associate|@DISEASE_Neoplasms|@GENE_PDCD1"}]},{"_id":"34562519|None","id":"34562519","infons":{},"passages":[{"infons":{"journal":"Cancer Lett. 2021 Dec 1;522:184-197. doi: 10.1016/j.canlet.2021.09.028. Epub 2021 ","year":"2021","type":"title","authors":"Shi W, Yang X, Xie S, Zhong D, Lin X, Ding Z, Duan S, Mo F, Liu A, Yin S, Jiang X, Xu ZPG, Lu X"},"offset":0,"text":"A new PD-1-specific nanobody enhances the antitumor activity of T-cells in synergy with dendritic cell vaccine.","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"1","infons":{"identifier":"5133","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"3681","valid":true,"normalized":[5133],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":5133,"biotype":"gene","name":"PDCD1","accession":"@GENE_PDCD1"},"text":"PD-1","locations":[{"offset":6,"length":4}]}],"relations":[]},{"infons":{"type":"abstract"},"offset":112,"text":"Despite the many successes and opportunities presented by PD-1 blockade in cancer therapies, anti-PD-1 monoclonal antibodies still face multiple challenges. Herein we report a strategy based on a nanobody (Nb) to circumvent these obstacles. A new PD-1-blocking Nb (PD-1 Nb20) in combination with tumor-specific dendritic cell (DC)/tumor-fusion cell (FC) vaccine that aims to improve the activation, proliferation, cytokine secretion, and tumor cell cytotoxicity of CD8+ T-cells. This combination was found to effectively enhance the in vitro cytotoxicity of CD8+ T-cells to kill human non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) HCC827 cells, hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) HepG2 cells, and tongue squamous cell carcinoma (TSCC) Tca8113 cells. Moreover, CD8+ T-cells pre-treated with PD-1 Nb20 and tumor-specific DC/tumor-FCs significantly suppressed the growth of NSCLC-, HCC- and TSCC-derived xenograft tumors and prolonged the survival of tumor-bearing mice, through promoting T-cell infiltration to kill tumor cells and inhibiting tumor angiogenesis. These data demonstrate that PD-1 Nb20 in synergy with DC/tumor-FC vaccine augment the broad spectrum of antitumor activity of CD8+ T-cells, providing an alternative and promising immunotherapeutic strategy for tumor patients who are T-cell-dysfunctional or not sensitive to anti-PD-1 therapy.","sentences":[],"annotations":[{"id":"43","infons":{"identifier":"5133","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"3681","valid":true,"normalized":[5133],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":5133,"biotype":"gene","name":"PDCD1","accession":"@GENE_PDCD1"},"text":"PD-1","locations":[{"offset":170,"length":4}]},{"id":"44","infons":{"identifier":"MESH:D009369","type":"Disease","valid":true,"normalized":["D009369"],"database":"ncbi_mesh","normalized_id":"D009369","biotype":"disease","name":"Neoplasms","accession":"@DISEASE_Neoplasms"},"text":"cancer","locations":[{"offset":187,"length":6}]},{"id":"45","infons":{"identifier":"5133","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"3681","valid":true,"normalized":[5133],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":5133,"biotype":"gene","name":"PDCD1","accession":"@GENE_PDCD1"},"text":"PD-1","locations":[{"offset":210,"length":4}]},{"id":"46","infons":{"identifier":"5133","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"3681","valid":true,"normalized":[5133],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":5133,"biotype":"gene","name":"PDCD1","accession":"@GENE_PDCD1"},"text":"PD-1","locations":[{"offset":359,"length":4}]},{"id":"47","infons":{"identifier":"5133","type":"Gene","ncbi_homologene":"3681","valid":true,"normalized":[5133],"database":"ncbi_gene","normalized_id":5133,"biotype":"gene","name":"PDCD1","accession":"@GENE_PDCD1"},"text":"PD-1","locations":[{"offset":377,"length":4}]},{"id":"48","infons":{"identifier"